// =============================================================================
// Prisma is the migration runner here. It is not the schema owner.
// =============================================================================
//
// This file has no models in it, and that is on purpose.
//
// Kitta's schema is mostly things a Prisma model cannot express: row level
// security policies, plpgsql triggers and functions, PostGIS geography and
// ltree columns, partial unique indexes, CHECK constraints written in SQL. If
// Prisma owned the schema, `prisma migrate dev` would diff its model of the
// database against the real one, decide the parts it cannot see are drift, and
// write a migration that drops them. That is not a hypothetical: RLS policies
// and triggers are exactly what a naive diff removes.
//
// So the arrangement is:
//
//   supabase/migrations/*.sql   the source of truth, written by hand
//   prisma/migrations/          generated copies, in the layout Prisma wants
//   prisma migrate deploy       applies whatever has not been applied yet
//
// Prisma contributes the two things that were actually missing: a record of
// which migrations a given database has already had (`_prisma_migrations`), and
// a command that applies the rest without anyone pasting SQL into a web editor.
//
// `npm run db:pull:remote` will fill this file in with models introspected from
// the live database if you want them for reference. Treat anything it writes as
// documentation, never as the definition — and note that the app itself does
// not query through Prisma. Every read and write goes through Supabase with the
// user's own session so that RLS applies; a Prisma client would hold the
// service role and quietly bypass all of it.
// =============================================================================

// The connection URL is deliberately absent: Prisma 7 removed `url` from schema
// files, and it now lives in prisma.config.ts, which reads DATABASE_URL out of
// whichever env file the npm script loaded.
datasource db {
  provider = "postgresql"
}
